{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And now, O Israel, what… but to fear…</b> The intention of the verse is along this line: for there are two levels that HaShem desires Israel to do, and one is above the other — one is fear (yir'ah), and above it is love (ahavah). And He said to them that He asks of them nothing but fear, and the reason for this is that fear will surround you to walk in all His ways, for one who fears will not cease from his fear even in one of a thousand things incumbent upon him to do. And not only does he arrive at this, but further he has ״to love Him״ — see that fear is the doorway to enter the gate of love. And therefore when the verse says <b>what does HaShem ask but to fear</b>, it was precise to say <b>and now</b> — meaning: what He asks, to fear, is for now, at this time when you come to take upon yourself the yoke of divinity; but this is not the ultimate goal of what He desires from us to do, except that this will stand us in the category of love. And along this way the statement <b>what</b> etc. <b>but</b> etc. will be settled, for relative to love, which is the expected thing, fear will be a minor matter. And in the Gemara where they said (Megillah 25a): 'Is fear then a minor matter?' etc. — the intention is that since relative to what one who attains it is great, it is not called small, even though relative to love it is below it; and they answered it with respect to Moses etc., that even relative to attainments it too is in the category of small." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained along this line: for it may arise in a person's mind that one who transgresses the word of HaShem — it is wondrous for him to attain His blessed will; therefore He said to him <b>and now</b> etc. — meaning, ״and now״ is an expression of repentance, as they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 21:6): ״and now״ is nothing but repentance — ״what does HaShem your Elohim ask of you״ to rectify your backsliding but fear alone; through this HaShem your Elohim will be appeased toward you." "And His saying <b>to walk in all His ways</b> etc. — this is according to what they of blessed memory said (Leviticus Rabbah 21:5): If you made wounds of transgressions, make opposite them wounds of commandments. And the Rambam wrote in his Commentary on the Mishnah (end of tractate Makkot): that if a person does not anger his Creator through transgressions, even if he performs only one commandment, there is power in that commandment to merit him for this world and the world to come, end quote. And I compared this matter to two servants of a king: one of them never angered the king all his days, and came to see the king's face with a young dove as an offering; and the other angered the king and came to see the king's face with one ox. There are circumstances where the king would rejoice at the offering of the bird and be angered at the offering of the ox, on account of the provocations that preceded from him. And that is what He says here to the penitent: <b>to walk in all His ways</b> etc. — to make wound after wound of commandments." "<b>Further,</b> the verse may intend the mystical secrets of HaShem according to words of truth, for through the mediation of the act of the lower ones the branches of holiness are unified, and likewise the reverse, as in the sod of (Proverbs 16:28) ״ונרגן מפריד אלוף״ — 'a whisperer separates the Aluf,' the Aluf of the world. And He comes to allude here to the matter that comes from a person's act, and that is His saying <b>what does HaShem your Elohim ask</b> etc. — meaning, the aspect of holiness called ״מה״ (mah), which is the Shekhinah, as they said in the Gemara (Sotah 11a) regarding the verse ״ותתצב אחתו״ etc., that the entire verse speaks of the Shekhinah, and this is its language: ״מה״ as written ״ועתה ישראל מה״ etc. — see that from this itself they prove that ״מה״ is the Shekhinah." "<b>And this</b> agrees and proceeds as well with what our Rabbis of blessed memory alerted us in their words where they said (Tanhuma, Korach 12): do not read ״מה״ (mah) but rather ״מאה״ (me'ah) — these are one hundred blessings, which is the sod of the totality of the decades; and He joined to their mention His blessed name, the Name of YHWH, which is the unification of the Holy One, blessed be He, and His Shekhinah — this is the asking of your Elohim from you. And it was precise to say <b>and now</b> to allude that there is no power in man to attain doing this except in this world; but after his death, even if he would do wondrously, it would produce no effect, and he would be like one of the angels. And we find that our Rabbis of blessed memory likened this world to an hour, as they said (Shabbat 33b): they abandon eternal life and engage in the life of the hour. And along this way His saying <b>but to fear</b> will be settled as a kind of hyle (matter), for relative to what is grasped in the intellect, which is great, the effect is diminished, even though tremendous exertion is needed to attain it; with all this, that which results from the toil is wondrous."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:42.930388561Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}